\title{The Mathematics of Jigsaw Puzzles: Edge Complementation, Boundary Topology, and the Satisfiability Correspondence}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous account of the classical jigsaw-puzzle assembly problem and
establish its exact relationship to Boolean satisfiability. Modeling each piece
edge as one of three shapes---\emph{flat}, \emph{tab}, or \emph{blank}---we define
a \emph{complementation} operation capturing which edges physically interlock and
show it to be an involution generating an order-two symmetry group on the edge
alphabet. We prove that the self-complementary edges (the fixed points of this
symmetry) are precisely the flat border edges, giving a topological reading of a
puzzle's outline as a fixed-point set. Encoding truth values as edge shapes
($\text{true}\mapsto\text{tab}$, $\text{false}\mapsto\text{blank}$), we prove a
\emph{local dictionary}: a clause piece's literal input interlocks with a
variable's output edge if and only if that literal is satisfied. Lifting this atom
through disjunction and conjunction yields the main correspondence---the puzzle
built from a formula assembles if and only if the formula is satisfiable. We cast
the construction as a genuine many-one (Karp) reduction of the satisfiability
language into the puzzle-assembly language, from which hardness transfers by
transitivity; the constructed instance uses exactly $2n+m+2$ pieces for $n$
variables and $m$ clauses. We conclude with a worked satisfiable instance and a
provably unsolvable instance, and discuss algorithmic and topological extensions.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The jigsaw puzzle is among the most familiar combinatorial objects, yet its
computational and algebraic structure is surprisingly rich. This paper isolates
three intertwined themes:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Boundary topology of edges.} The mating relation between piece
  edges is governed by a single involution whose fixed-point set is exactly the
  border of the assembled figure.
  \item \textbf{The assembly--satisfiability dictionary.} By encoding truth values
  as edge shapes, we identify placement of a clause piece with satisfaction of a
  clause, and hence solvability of a whole puzzle with satisfiability of a formula.
  \item \textbf{The reduction as a many-one reduction.} The construction is a Karp
  reduction of Boolean satisfiability into puzzle assembly, so puzzle assembly
  inherits the full hardness of satisfiability.
\end{enumerate}
The unifying object is a single order-two symmetry: edge complementation. Its
reversibility carries the logic, and its fixed points carry the topology.
Throughout, a puzzle is specified abstractly by the formula it encodes; this keeps
the reduction map transparent while preserving the full logical content.

\section{Edges and the algebra of complementation}

\begin{definition}[Edge]
The \emph{edge alphabet} is the three-element set
$\Edge = \{\text{flat},\ \text{tab},\ \text{blank}\}$. A \emph{flat} edge is a
straight border edge; a \emph{tab} protrudes outward; a \emph{blank} recedes
inward.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Complementation]
The \emph{complement} map $\comp\colon \Edge\to\Edge$ is
\[
\comp(\text{flat}) = \text{flat}, \qquad \comp(\text{tab}) = \text{blank},
\qquad \comp(\text{blank}) = \text{tab}.
\]
Intuitively, $\comp(e)$ is the unique shape that physically interlocks with an
edge of shape $e$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Involutivity]
For every edge $e$, $\comp(\comp(e)) = e$. Consequently $\comp$ is a bijection,
in fact its own inverse.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Check the three cases. Flat is fixed; tab $\mapsto$ blank $\mapsto$ tab; blank
$\mapsto$ tab $\mapsto$ blank. In each case two applications return the input, and
an involution is injective and surjective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The complementation symmetry]
The map $\comp$ is a permutation of $\Edge$ with $\comp^2 = \mathrm{id}$, and it
generates a cyclic group of order two, $\langle\comp\rangle \cong
\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$, acting on the edge alphabet.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary $=$ fixed points]\label{thm:boundary}
An edge is self-complementary if and only if it is flat:
$\comp(e) = e \iff e = \text{flat}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $e = \text{flat}$ then $\comp(e) = \text{flat} = e$. Conversely
$\comp(\text{tab}) = \text{blank}\ne\text{tab}$ and
$\comp(\text{blank}) = \text{tab}\ne\text{blank}$, so no non-flat edge is fixed.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Interpretation.} The flat edges are precisely the edges on the
boundary of an assembled figure. Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary} identifies the outline
of the assembled picture with the fixed-point set of the order-two complementation
symmetry: the border is the invariant locus of a symmetry.

\section{Interlocking edges}

\begin{definition}[Fits]
Edge $a$ \emph{fits} edge $b$, written $a\fitsym b$, when $b = \comp(a)$; that is,
when the two sides physically interlock.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetry of fitting]
If $a\fitsym b$ then $b\fitsym a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $b = \comp(a)$ then $\comp(b) = \comp(\comp(a)) = a$ by involutivity, so
$a = \comp(b)$, i.e.\ $b\fitsym a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of partner]
If $a\fitsym b$ and $a\fitsym b'$ then $b = b'$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both equal $\comp(a)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Encoding truth values as edges}

\begin{definition}[Truth encoding]
The encoding $\enc\colon\{\text{true},\text{false}\}\to\Edge$ is
$\enc(\text{true}) = \text{tab}$, $\enc(\text{false}) = \text{blank}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Injectivity of the encoding]\label{thm:encinj}
$\enc$ is injective: an edge determines the truth value it carries.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The two inputs map to the distinct outputs tab and blank.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Variable mutual exclusion]
$\enc(\text{true})\ne\enc(\text{false})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate: tab $\ne$ blank.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Interpretation.} A variable gadget offers a TRUE piece exposing a
tab and a FALSE piece exposing a blank on a shared assignment channel. Since the
two shapes differ, at most one piece can occupy the channel; mutual exclusion of
truth values is realized geometrically.

\section{Formulas, assignments, and the assembly dictionary}

\begin{definition}[Syntax]
A \emph{literal} is a pair $\ell = (v,p)\in\mathbb{N}\times\{\text{true},
\text{false}\}$. A \emph{clause} is a finite list of literals (their disjunction);
a \emph{formula} is a finite list of clauses (their conjunction, i.e.\ conjunctive
normal form). An \emph{assignment} is a function
$a\colon\mathbb{N}\to\{\text{true},\text{false}\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Satisfaction]
A literal $(v,p)$ is \emph{satisfied} by $a$ when $a(v) = p$; a clause is
satisfied when some literal is; a formula is satisfied when every clause is; and a
formula is \emph{satisfiable} when some assignment satisfies it.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Literal fit]
The clause piece for literal $\ell = (v,p)$ exposes input edge $\comp(\enc(p))$.
Under $a$, $\ell$ \emph{fits} when $\enc(a(v))\fitsym\comp(\enc(p))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Local dictionary]\label{thm:local}
For every assignment $a$ and literal $\ell$, $\ell$ fits under $a$ if and only if
$\ell$ is satisfied by $a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $\ell = (v,p)$. By definition of $\fitsym$, $\ell$ fits iff
$\comp(\enc(p)) = \comp(\enc(a(v)))$. Since $\comp$ is injective, this is
equivalent to $\enc(p) = \enc(a(v))$; and since $\enc$ is injective
(Theorem~\ref{thm:encinj}), to $a(v) = p$, i.e.\ $\ell$ is satisfied. Conversely,
substituting $a(v) = p$ makes both sides equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Placement, assembly, solvability]
A clause piece for $c$ can be \emph{placed} under $a$ when some $\ell\in c$ fits.
The puzzle built from $F$ is \emph{assembled} under $a$ when every clause piece
can be placed, and \emph{solvable} when assembled under some assignment.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Clause dictionary]\label{thm:clause}
A clause piece for $c$ can be placed under $a$ if and only if $c$ is satisfied
by $a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both ask for some $\ell\in c$; these match literal-by-literal by
Theorem~\ref{thm:local}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Main correspondence]\label{thm:main}
The puzzle built from a formula $F$ is solvable if and only if $F$ is satisfiable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Solvability provides an assignment placing every clause piece; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:clause} that assignment satisfies every clause, hence $F$.
Conversely a satisfying assignment places every clause piece by
Theorem~\ref{thm:clause}, assembling the puzzle.
\end{proof}

\section{The piece count of the construction}

\begin{definition}[The piece set]
For $n$ variables and formula $F$ with $m = |F|$ clauses, the construction
assembles two corner pieces (enforcing the boundary), two variable pieces per
variable (a TRUE tab piece and a FALSE blank piece), and one clause piece per
clause.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Piece count]
The construction produces exactly $2n + m + 2$ pieces.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Two corners, $2n$ variable pieces, and $m$ clause pieces sum to $2n + m + 2$.
\end{proof}

\section{The reduction and hardness transfer}

\begin{definition}[Many-one reducibility]
For languages $A\subseteq\alpha$ and $B\subseteq\beta$, $A$ \emph{many-one (Karp)
reduces} to $B$, written $A\le_m B$, when there is $f\colon\alpha\to\beta$ with
$x\in A \iff f(x)\in B$ for all $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Transitivity]\label{thm:trans}
If $A\le_m B$ and $B\le_m C$ then $A\le_m C$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $f,g$ witness the hypotheses, $g\circ f$ witnesses $A\le_m C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[The two languages]
Let $\SAT = \{F : F \text{ satisfiable}\}$ and
$\JIG = \{F : \text{the puzzle built from } F \text{ is solvable}\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The reduction]\label{thm:red}
$\SAT \le_m \JIG$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $f$ to be the construction sending a formula to its puzzle. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, $F\in\SAT \iff f(F)\in\JIG$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hardness transfer]
For every language $L$ with $L\le_m\SAT$, also $L\le_m\JIG$. In particular, puzzle
assembly inherits the full hardness of Boolean satisfiability.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Compose with Theorem~\ref{thm:red} using transitivity
(Theorem~\ref{thm:trans}).
\end{proof}

Since Boolean satisfiability is NP-complete, every problem in NP many-one reduces
to it, hence to puzzle assembly, which is therefore NP-hard.

\section{Worked instances}

\subsection*{A solvable instance}
Consider $F = (x_1\lor x_2\lor\lnot x_3)\land(\lnot x_1\lor x_3)$, with three
variables and two clauses; its puzzle uses $2\cdot 3 + 2 + 2 = 10$ pieces.

\begin{claim}
The puzzle for $F$ is solvable.
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
Take $a$ with $a(2) = \text{true}$ and $a(v) = \text{false}$ otherwise. The first
clause is satisfied by $(x_2,\text{true})$; the second by $(x_1,\text{false})$.
Thus $F$ is satisfiable and, by Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, the puzzle is solvable.
\end{proof}

\subsection*{An unsolvable instance}
Consider the contradictory $F' = x_1\land\lnot x_1$.

\begin{claim}
The puzzle for $F'$ is not solvable.
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
If some $a$ solved it, by Theorem~\ref{thm:main} it would satisfy $F'$, forcing
$a(1) = \text{true}$ and $a(1) = \text{false}$, whence $\text{true} =
\text{false}$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion and future directions}

A single order-two symmetry---edge complementation---supplies topology (its
fixed-point set is the boundary), logic (its reversibility drives the local
dictionary, whence the assembly--satisfiability correspondence), and complexity
(packaged as a many-one reduction, it imports the hardness of satisfiability). The
correspondence is \emph{local}: every global equivalence factors through the
single-literal fit lemma, whose only inputs are two injectivity facts.

Future directions include: (i) showing assembly hardness survives any bounded
palette of $k\ge 3$ interlock shapes, since hardness is carried by the symmetry of
the alphabet rather than its size; (ii) proving the exposed-edge multiset of any
valid assembly is supported on the fixed-point set of complementation, with
cardinality a topological invariant (boundary length) computable by Burnside-style
fixed-point counting; (iii) upgrading the correspondence to a bijection so that
uniqueness of assembly detects unique satisfiability and counting assemblies
counts satisfying assignments; and (iv) a calculus of composable gadgets that
reduces other combinatorial problems directly into puzzle assembly.

\section{Conclusion}
Beneath the pastime of assembling a jigsaw puzzle lies a compact mathematical
edifice: an involution that swaps tab and blank and fixes the flat border draws
the outline of the picture as its fixed-point set, encodes truth and falsehood in
its two moving points, and, as a many-one reduction, smuggles the hardness of
Boolean satisfiability into the act of completing a puzzle.

